{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 39, :comments ["<b>And it shall be for you for tzitzit.</b> Now the thread returns to being at the corner like the tzitzit. The second interpretation is as the Sages translated, and since there are reliable witnesses for the second interpretation, the first is nullified. They translated that this commandment applies to a garment that has four corners, and the tzitzit are the strands — and I shall explain further. Now it is a commandment upon everyone who has a garment with four corners to cover himself with it constantly by day and not remove it from himself, so that they remember. And those who pray in a tallit at the time of prayer do so because they recite in the recitation of Shema 'and it shall be for you for tzitzit,' and they make for themselves tzitzit. But in my opinion he is more obligated to wrap himself in tzitzit at all other hours than at the hour of prayer, so that he remember and not stray and not commit a transgression at any hour — for at the hour of prayer he will not commit a transgression." "<b>And you shall see it.</b> It is a commandment that it be visible." "<b>After your heart.</b> One who desires and the eye sees and the heart covets — now the tzitzit will be for a sign and a reminder, so that a person not pursue the musings of his heart and all that his eyes seek." "<b>That you go astray.</b> For whoever goes after his desire is a whore from under the service of his Elohim."], :book :numbers, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {0 "The phrase 'והנה ישוב הפתיל להיותו בקצה כמו הציצית' refers back to the preceding dispute (on v. 38) about the meaning of פתיל תכלת; rendered literally without expanding."}, :source-witnesses [["On Your Way" "http://mobile.tora.ws/"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:17:57.045000406Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}